1
00:00:00,361 --> 00:00:02,969
The iron oxide showed us that atoms combine

2
00:00:03,009 --> 00:00:04,633
by count, not by mass.

3
00:00:05,436 --> 00:00:06,301
Now look at a reaction.

4
00:00:07,006 --> 00:00:09,688
Two H two plus O two makes two

5
00:00:09,709 --> 00:00:10,529
H two O,

6
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:13,391
and the coefficients are two, one and two.

7
00:00:14,133 --> 00:00:17,055
So does two grams of hydrogen react with

8
00:00:17,196 --> 00:00:19,518
one gram of oxygen, to make two grams

9
00:00:19,579 --> 00:00:19,979
of water?

10
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:21,295
Yes or no?

11
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:22,360
And if not,

12
00:00:22,902 --> 00:00:25,645
what do the two, one and two actually

13
00:00:25,686 --> 00:00:25,966
count?

14
00:00:29,571 --> 00:00:29,732
No.

15
00:00:30,218 --> 00:00:31,812
Coefficients count particles.

16
00:00:32,438 --> 00:00:35,531
Two hydrogen molecules react with one oxygen molecule

17
00:00:35,833 --> 00:00:37,419
to make two water molecules.

18
00:00:38,042 --> 00:00:40,326
Count the atoms, and there are four hydrogens

19
00:00:40,548 --> 00:00:42,130
and two oxygens on each side,

20
00:00:42,692 --> 00:00:44,295
just as we balanced equations earlier.

21
00:00:45,321 --> 00:00:46,125
Now scale it up.

22
00:00:46,748 --> 00:00:49,935
Two dozen hydrogen molecules need one dozen oxygen

23
00:00:49,956 --> 00:00:50,417
molecules.

24
00:00:51,079 --> 00:00:53,583
And two moles need one mole, because a

25
00:00:53,644 --> 00:00:56,167
mole is just a very large count, six

26
00:00:56,208 --> 00:00:58,331
point zero two times ten to the twenty-three

27
00:00:58,432 --> 00:00:58,872
particles.

28
00:00:59,654 --> 00:01:02,582
So the coefficients give a mole ratio, two

29
00:01:02,603 --> 00:01:03,705
to one to two.

30
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:05,120
Now the masses.

31
00:01:05,769 --> 00:01:08,364
Two moles of hydrogen molecules weigh about four

32
00:01:08,425 --> 00:01:08,766
grams.

33
00:01:09,351 --> 00:01:11,980
One mole of oxygen weighs thirty-two grams.

34
00:01:12,423 --> 00:01:14,811
And two moles of water weigh about thirty-six

35
00:01:14,872 --> 00:01:15,172
grams.

36
00:01:16,056 --> 00:01:18,640
So the mass ratio is roughly four to

37
00:01:18,701 --> 00:01:20,123
thirty-two to thirty-six,

38
00:01:20,766 --> 00:01:22,729
or about one to eight to nine.

39
00:01:23,492 --> 00:01:25,375
That's nothing like two to one to two,

40
00:01:25,797 --> 00:01:28,581
because each oxygen molecule is about sixteen times

41
00:01:28,663 --> 00:01:30,465
heavier than a hydrogen molecule.

42
00:01:31,036 --> 00:01:32,443
But notice what does hold.

43
00:01:33,026 --> 00:01:35,652
Four point zero three plus thirty-two point zero

44
00:01:35,673 --> 00:01:38,018
zero is thirty-six point zero three.

45
00:01:38,721 --> 00:01:41,249
Mass is conserved, because the same atoms are

46
00:01:41,270 --> 00:01:42,132
just rearranged.

47
00:01:42,897 --> 00:01:45,732
So a balanced equation promises two things.

48
00:01:46,437 --> 00:01:49,007
Moles react in the ratio of the coefficients,

49
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:51,418
and the total mass stays the same.

50
00:01:52,041 --> 00:01:54,408
It never promises that masses react in the

51
00:01:54,449 --> 00:01:55,531
ratio of the coefficients.

52
00:01:56,297 --> 00:01:57,308
Here's a picture that helps.

53
00:01:57,956 --> 00:01:59,929
To build a bike, you need two wheels

54
00:02:00,091 --> 00:02:00,875
for every frame.

55
00:02:01,461 --> 00:02:03,239
That's a count ratio, two to one.

56
00:02:03,963 --> 00:02:06,087
But nobody says you need two kilograms of

57
00:02:06,149 --> 00:02:07,952
wheels for every kilogram of frame.

58
00:02:08,715 --> 00:02:09,978
The recipe counts parts,

59
00:02:10,481 --> 00:02:12,386
and their masses depend on what each part

60
00:02:12,467 --> 00:02:12,847
weighs.

61
00:02:13,651 --> 00:02:15,883
A chemical equation works the same way, with

62
00:02:15,985 --> 00:02:17,070
moles as the count.

63
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:18,467
One trap.

64
00:02:19,056 --> 00:02:21,330
In two H two O, there are two

65
00:02:21,432 --> 00:02:22,216
different twos.

66
00:02:22,819 --> 00:02:24,964
The big two in front is the coefficient,

67
00:02:25,306 --> 00:02:26,609
and it counts water molecules.

68
00:02:27,251 --> 00:02:28,895
The small two is the subscript,

69
00:02:29,417 --> 00:02:32,305
and it counts hydrogen atoms inside one molecule.

70
00:02:33,231 --> 00:02:35,464
Mole ratios come only from the coefficients.

71
00:02:36,028 --> 00:02:40,251
And when balancing, change coefficients, never subscripts, because

72
00:02:40,292 --> 00:02:42,553
changing a subscript makes a different substance.

73
00:02:43,417 --> 00:02:45,491
Using a ratio is just scaling.

74
00:02:46,237 --> 00:02:48,079
In two A l plus three C l

75
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:50,283
two, making two A l C l three,

76
00:02:50,805 --> 00:02:53,209
aluminium and chlorine react two to three.

77
00:02:53,731 --> 00:02:53,890
So

78
00:02:54,392 --> 00:02:57,076
zero point four zero moles of aluminium needs

79
00:02:57,117 --> 00:02:59,801
zero point four zero times three over two,

80
00:03:00,162 --> 00:03:02,044
which is zero point six zero moles of

81
00:03:02,105 --> 00:03:02,485
chlorine.

82
00:03:03,388 --> 00:03:06,675
Wanted equals given, times the wanted coefficient over

83
00:03:06,716 --> 00:03:07,617
the given coefficient.

84
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:10,244
Flip the fraction by mistake, and you'd get

85
00:03:10,325 --> 00:03:12,671
zero point two seven, which is the ratio

86
00:03:12,772 --> 00:03:13,232
upside down.

87
00:03:13,881 --> 00:03:16,568
But how do we know reactions really follow

88
00:03:16,729 --> 00:03:18,153
whole-number mole ratios?

89
00:03:18,858 --> 00:03:19,466
We can test it.

90
00:03:20,235 --> 00:03:23,098
In the continuous variation method, we mix two

91
00:03:23,158 --> 00:03:25,099
reactants in changing proportions,

92
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:27,321
always with the same total volume,

93
00:03:27,862 --> 00:03:29,883
and measure something that shows how much reaction

94
00:03:29,903 --> 00:03:30,224
happened.

95
00:03:31,026 --> 00:03:33,450
For hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide,

96
00:03:33,973 --> 00:03:36,678
that's the temperature rise, because the reaction releases

97
00:03:36,739 --> 00:03:36,939
heat.

98
00:03:37,688 --> 00:03:40,500
Both solutions have the same concentration, so every

99
00:03:40,541 --> 00:03:42,509
millilitre holds the same number of moles.

100
00:03:43,073 --> 00:03:45,282
That makes a volume ratio a mole ratio.

101
00:03:45,963 --> 00:03:48,364
We mix five millilitres of acid with thirty-five

102
00:03:48,385 --> 00:03:50,586
of base, then ten and thirty, and so

103
00:03:50,667 --> 00:03:52,707
on, always forty millilitres in total.

104
00:03:53,469 --> 00:03:55,760
Keeping the total fixed means the only thing

105
00:03:55,801 --> 00:03:57,750
that changes is how the moles are split.

106
00:03:58,313 --> 00:04:00,499
Safety glasses go on, because both solutions are

107
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:02,345
corrosive or irritating at this strength.

108
00:04:03,088 --> 00:04:06,200
The temperature rise climbs, peaks at twenty millilitres

109
00:04:06,222 --> 00:04:07,707
of each, then falls again.

110
00:04:08,454 --> 00:04:09,125
Why a peak?

111
00:04:09,913 --> 00:04:12,023
With too little acid, some base is left

112
00:04:12,125 --> 00:04:13,370
over with nothing to react with.

113
00:04:13,914 --> 00:04:15,850
With too much acid, it's the other way

114
00:04:15,891 --> 00:04:16,153
round.

115
00:04:17,098 --> 00:04:19,746
Only at the right ratio is everything used

116
00:04:19,827 --> 00:04:22,234
up, so that mixture releases the most heat.

117
00:04:23,118 --> 00:04:25,422
Draw a straight line through each side, and

118
00:04:25,443 --> 00:04:26,925
where they cross is the ratio,

119
00:04:27,427 --> 00:04:29,511
twenty to twenty, or one to one.

120
00:04:30,333 --> 00:04:32,537
That crossing is better evidence than the single

121
00:04:32,638 --> 00:04:35,483
highest point, because the true ratio could fall

122
00:04:35,524 --> 00:04:36,625
between two mixtures.

123
00:04:37,453 --> 00:04:37,919
Your turn.

124
00:04:38,528 --> 00:04:40,953
The same method is used with sulfuric acid

125
00:04:41,194 --> 00:04:44,260
and sodium hydroxide, both at the same concentration,

126
00:04:44,983 --> 00:04:46,425
thirty millilitres in total.

127
00:04:47,148 --> 00:04:50,016
The biggest temperature rise comes near ten millilitres

128
00:04:50,077 --> 00:04:50,597
of acid.

129
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:53,341
Use the data to find the mole ratio,

130
00:04:53,643 --> 00:04:55,063
and write the balanced equation.

131
00:04:55,826 --> 00:04:56,800
Pause here to answer.

132
00:05:07,287 --> 00:05:09,872
The lines through each side cross at ten

133
00:05:09,894 --> 00:05:12,519
millilitres of acid and twenty of base.

134
00:05:13,222 --> 00:05:16,049
Equal concentrations mean that's a mole ratio of

135
00:05:16,271 --> 00:05:16,731
one to two.

136
00:05:17,735 --> 00:05:17,894
So

137
00:05:18,456 --> 00:05:20,399
H two S O four plus two N

138
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:22,283
a O H makes N a two S

139
00:05:22,564 --> 00:05:24,246
O four plus two H two O.

140
00:05:25,230 --> 00:05:27,480
Saying one to one, because the concentrations were

141
00:05:27,602 --> 00:05:29,410
equal, is the classic slip.

142
00:05:29,994 --> 00:05:32,582
Equal concentrations make volumes count moles.

143
00:05:33,125 --> 00:05:35,083
They don't make the ratio one to one.

144
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:37,287
Last one.

145
00:05:38,015 --> 00:05:41,515
For N two plus three H two making

146
00:05:41,756 --> 00:05:42,836
two N H three,

147
00:05:43,557 --> 00:05:46,117
a student says twenty-eight point zero grams of

148
00:05:46,218 --> 00:05:50,198
nitrogen needs three times twenty-eight point zero, or

149
00:05:50,339 --> 00:05:52,419
eighty-four point zero grams of hydrogen.

150
00:05:53,223 --> 00:05:54,112
Evaluate this claim.

151
00:05:55,003 --> 00:05:55,970
Pause here to answer.

152
00:06:06,651 --> 00:06:07,584
The claim is wrong.

153
00:06:08,129 --> 00:06:10,977
Twenty-eight point zero grams of nitrogen is almost

154
00:06:11,098 --> 00:06:12,381
exactly one mole.

155
00:06:13,083 --> 00:06:15,605
The equation says one mole of nitrogen needs

156
00:06:15,726 --> 00:06:18,267
three moles of hydrogen, and three moles of

157
00:06:18,468 --> 00:06:20,569
H two is only about six grams.

158
00:06:21,372 --> 00:06:23,541
The student treated the coefficients as a mass

159
00:06:23,602 --> 00:06:23,983
ratio.

160
00:06:24,589 --> 00:06:25,802
They're a mole ratio.

161
00:06:27,428 --> 00:06:30,401
So coefficients count particles, and that makes them

162
00:06:30,663 --> 00:06:31,586
mole ratios.

163
00:06:32,291 --> 00:06:35,025
Masses follow from moles, and the total mass

164
00:06:35,126 --> 00:06:35,849
is conserved.

165
00:06:36,494 --> 00:06:38,822
Experiments confirm the whole-number ratios.

166
00:06:39,768 --> 00:06:42,500
Now we have a model, moles, molar mass

167
00:06:42,862 --> 00:06:43,565
and ratios.

168
00:06:44,189 --> 00:06:46,164
Can it solve a problem we haven't seen?

169
00:06:47,031 --> 00:06:49,736
An old jar of copper sulfate crystals loses

170
00:06:49,857 --> 00:06:52,062
only thirty point two percent of its mass.

171
00:06:53,026 --> 00:06:55,558
How can a chemistry model solve an unfamiliar

172
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:56,644
quantitative problem?

173
00:06:59,295 --> 00:07:00,158
Here are the key notes.

174
00:07:00,561 --> 00:07:02,108
Pause the video if you need more time

175
00:07:02,150 --> 00:07:02,611
to copy them.
